    <item>
      <title>Vulkan render loop synchronization</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2024-01-24-vulkan-loop/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 24 Jan 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2024-01-24-vulkan-loop/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;One of the things I struggled the most when learning Vulkan is the &lt;strong&gt;render loop synchronization&lt;/strong&gt;. For over 2 years I&amp;rsquo;ve been revisiting the topic many times. Every time, I though I had finally understood how to do it properly. But I see different approaches implemented in tutorials and examples out there, and some of them are buggy or sub-optimal.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In this post I wanted to write down the way I settled with. This way is correct (no validation errors), optimal (no unnecessary stalls), and simple.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ImGui gamma correction in OpenGL and Vulkan</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2022-11-24-imgui-gamma/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 24 Nov 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2022-11-24-imgui-gamma/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I had been using ImGui with OpenGL for a long time. Never encountered any problem with gamma corection.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Recently, I have started using ImGui wih Vulkan and noticed that the colors looked brighter. I immediately suspected it was a gamma issue.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Googling how to solve my problem, I found lots of people with similar experiences(&lt;sup id=&#34;fnref:1&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#fn:1&#34; class=&#34;footnote-ref&#34; role=&#34;doc-noteref&#34;&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;, &lt;sup id=&#34;fnref:2&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#fn:2&#34; class=&#34;footnote-ref&#34; role=&#34;doc-noteref&#34;&gt;2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;, &lt;sup id=&#34;fnref:3&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#fn:3&#34; class=&#34;footnote-ref&#34; role=&#34;doc-noteref&#34;&gt;3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#the-actual-good-fix&#34;&gt;SKIP BLAHBLAH AND GO TO THE GOOD SOLUTION&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;dumb-solution&#34;&gt;Dumb solution&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One of the solutions people suggested is to create the swapchain images in linear RGB, instead of sRGB. That&amp;rsquo;s obviously not a good solution, because our own application beneffits from using sRGB compression to avoid banding issues. But it does indeed fix the issue with ImGui so it confirms out suspicions about gamma correction being the culprit.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lightnings!</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2022-10-12-lightnings/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 12 Oct 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2022-10-12-lightnings/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I have made a weapon in &lt;a href=&#34;https://twitter.com/robots_evil&#34;&gt;Evil Robots&lt;/a&gt; that casts lightnings to nearby enemies.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div style=&#34;position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden;&#34;&gt;&#xA;      &lt;iframe allow=&#34;accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen&#34; loading=&#34;eager&#34; referrerpolicy=&#34;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&#34; src=&#34;https://www.youtube.com/embed/P_t0vi-utS0?autoplay=0&amp;amp;controls=1&amp;amp;end=0&amp;amp;loop=0&amp;amp;mute=0&amp;amp;start=0&#34; style=&#34;position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border:0;&#34; title=&#34;YouTube video&#34;&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In this post I wanted to show you how I approached it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I made a demo that you can find in &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/tuket/lightning_demo&#34;&gt;github&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Eventhough my game uses OpenGL, for this demo I have used the &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/floooh/sokol&#34;&gt;sokol&lt;/a&gt; library. I chose to use this library because it&amp;rsquo;s a very simple and easy to use library, and it can be compiled to most platforms (including web). Besides I wanted an excuse to give it a try out and learn something new :).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Explosion Decals</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2022-08-05-explosion-decals/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 05 Aug 2022 21:15:50 +0200</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2022-08-05-explosion-decals/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m going to explain a technique that I implemented for the explosion decals of my game.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Demo&lt;/strong&gt; available in this &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/tuket/explosion_decals&#34;&gt;github repo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;  &lt;img src=&#34;https://tuket.github.io/img/explosion_decals/demo.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;demo.jpg&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Many games have the need to splat textures on to surfaces dynamically. For example, we might have a floor and we would like to splat some blood onto it when a character has been hurt.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;  &lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img src=&#34;https://www.gamemodd.com/uploads/posts/2017-07/1500927041_f.e.a.r.-blood.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;d&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;Image from https://www.gamemodd.com/cs/sprites/blooddecals/3926-fear-blood.html&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The common approach for implementing these so called &lt;strong&gt;decals&lt;/strong&gt; is to project a quad along a direction. There needs to be a near-far range, so the decal only affects an enclosing box.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Circle vs Ray intersection</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2020-07-09-circle-vs-ray-intersection/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2020-07-09-circle-vs-ray-intersection/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;A simple way to compute the intersection of a ray vs a circle.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;img src=&#34;https://tuket.github.io/img/ray_circle_intersect.svg&#34; alt=&#34;ray_circle_intersect.svg&#34; width=&#34;100%&#34;/&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Code &lt;a href=&#34;https://gist.github.com/tuket/7f604c2ea16f62af5a0d4778c80ac8c9&#34; title=&#34;snippet&#34;&gt;snippet&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-cpp&#34; data-lang=&#34;cpp&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nf&#34;&gt;rayVsCircle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;depth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;glm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vec2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;rayOrig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;glm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vec2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;rayDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;glm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vec2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;circlePos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;circleRad&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;R&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;circleRad&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;glm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vec2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;op&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;circlePos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;rayOrig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;dot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;op&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;op&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;// is rayOrigin inside the circle ?&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;        &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;depth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;        &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nb&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;D&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;dot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;rayDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;op&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;H2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;dot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;op&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;op&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;K2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;R&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;H2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;K2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;        &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;depth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;D&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;sqrt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;K2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;        &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;depth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;            &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nb&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nb&#34;&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Demo here: &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/tuket/demo_ray_vs_circle/blob/master/README.md&#34;&gt;https://github.com/tuket/demo_ray_vs_circle/blob/master/README.md&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Generate icosphere mesh fast</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2020-06-10-generate-icosphere-mesh-fast/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 10 Jun 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2020-06-10-generate-icosphere-mesh-fast/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;If you seach in the Internet for algorithms to generate icosphere meshes, most algorithms out there take an icosahedron and keep tessellating the triangular faces recursivelly (&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Spherical-tessellation-grids-and-parent-daughter-triangle-relationships-The-initial_fig5_253252594&#34; title=&#34;link&#34;&gt;link&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;img src=&#34;https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Nathan_Simmons/publication/253252594/figure/fig5/AS:668851566043151@1536478048938/Spherical-tessellation-grids-and-parent-daughter-triangle-relationships-The-initial.png&#34; alt=&#34;recursive icosphere tesellation&#34; width=&#34;50%&#34;/&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have made an algorithm that outputs the result, in a couple of flat arrays(vertices and indices), in a single pass, so it&amp;rsquo;s faster and very cache friendly. It also has the advantage that you can query the exact memory you need to allocate.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Compute triangle area from three 2D points (fast way)</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2020-02-14-compute-triangle-area-from-three-2d-points/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 14 Feb 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2020-02-14-compute-triangle-area-from-three-2d-points/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;TLDR: code is at the end.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It&amp;rsquo;s well know that we can compute the area of a triangle as the base times the height divided by two.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;$$&#xA;A = 0.5 \cdot b \cdot h&#xA;$$&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;img src=&#34;https://tuket.github.io/img/tria_area/tria_area_bh.png&#34; alt=&#34;tria_area_bh.png&#34; width=&#34;40%&#34;/&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But how do we compute the area given the coordinates of the points?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One efficient way is using the dot product.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We make the vectors $\vec{v_1}$ and $\vec{v_2}$ by linking one point to the other two.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>C&#43;&#43; virtual inheritance diagrams and links</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2019-05-12-c-virtual-inheritance-diagrams-and-links/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 12 May 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2019-05-12-c-virtual-inheritance-diagrams-and-links/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;These is a cheatsheet of the memory layout of some common pattens in virtual inheritance.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For each case there is first the code.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Then there is a diagram.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;On the left there is a representation of the inheritance relationships.&#xA;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#A59200&#34;&gt;Gold&lt;/span&gt; color means virtual.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;On the right there is the memory layout.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;The dashed arrows represent where a pointer of that type would point if a casting was performed.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;At the bottom of this page there is a collection of &lt;a href=&#34;#links&#34;&gt;links&lt;/a&gt;. Some of them are good for a first contact, and other go very in depth.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>C&#43;&#43; inheritance under the hood</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2019-04-27-c-inheritance-under-the-hood/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 27 Apr 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2019-04-27-c-inheritance-under-the-hood/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In this post we are going to explore how C++ inheritance works.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We will cover things like the memory layout of the objects, vtables, different types of casting and virtual inheritance.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h1 id=&#34;simple-inheritance&#34;&gt;Simple inheritance&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s start with the simplest kind of inheritance: only one parent, no virtual functions, no virtual inheritance.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;img src=&#34;{{site.baseurl}}/img/simple.svg&#34; alt=&#34;simple.svg&#34; width=&#34;100&#34;/&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This would be the code.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-cpp&#34; data-lang=&#34;cpp&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nc&#34;&gt;Widget&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;widget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nc&#34;&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;Widget&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://ideone.com/2Wj0uo&#34;&gt;Try code&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And this would be the memory layout of an instance of &lt;code&gt;Button&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Nifty hidden shapes in a Codejam problem</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2018-10-13-nifty-hiddent-shapes-in-a-codejam-problem/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 13 Oct 2018 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2018-10-13-nifty-hiddent-shapes-in-a-codejam-problem/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I was trying to solve a problem called &lt;a href=&#34;https://codejam.withgoogle.com/codejam/contest/32002/dashboard&#34;&gt;&lt;em&gt;How Big Are the Pockets?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; from Codejam.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Basically they want you to count the number of shaded squares in a figure like follows.&#xA;&#xA;  &lt;img src=&#34;https://codejam.withgoogle.com/codejam/contest/images/?image=pockets02.png&amp;amp;p=24444&amp;amp;c=32002&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;They give you as input a sequence of movements (right, left, or forward):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;9&#xA;F 6 R 1 F 4 RFF 2 LFF 1&#xA;LFFFR 1 F 2 R 1 F 5&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;In order to debug my solution I made a &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/tuket/challenges/blob/master/codejam/08/3/a_view.cpp&#34;&gt;program&lt;/a&gt; that draws the line path and a trace of my solution.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Sending emails with C&#43;&#43; and GMail</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2018-09-03-send-gmail-cpp/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 03 Sep 2018 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2018-09-03-send-gmail-cpp/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In this post I&amp;rsquo;m going to show how to send emails using C++ and a GMail account.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For networking I&amp;rsquo;m going to be using Asio (non-boost verion).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you just want to see the code, here it is: &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/tuket/send_gmail&#34;&gt;github.com/tuket/send_gmail&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;ssl&#34;&gt;SSL&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You cannot simply connect to GMail through a regular socket. You will have to use SSL to stablish a secure connection. In Asio you will have to create an &lt;code&gt;ssl::context&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-c++&#34; data-lang=&#34;c++&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;asio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;ssl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;context&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;sslContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;asio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;ssl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;tlsv12_client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Then we have to load the certificates.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Sorting multiple vectors in the same way using C&#43;&#43; variadic templates</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-09-30-sorting-multiple-vectors-in-the-same-way-using-c-variadic-templates/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 30 Sep 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-09-30-sorting-multiple-vectors-in-the-same-way-using-c-variadic-templates/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s imagine we have a table like shown below. In this simple example we have information about people organized in rows.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;table&gt;&#xA;  &lt;thead&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;Id&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th&gt;Surname&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th&gt;Age&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/thead&gt;&#xA;  &lt;tbody&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;Tuket&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Troco&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;24&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;Rufol&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Conino&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;11&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;Muscul&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Man&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;20&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td style=&#34;text-align: center&#34;&gt;Jigoro&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Kano&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;78&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/tbody&gt;&#xA;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And we represent this table using C++ &lt;code&gt;std::vector&lt;/code&gt;s:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-cpp&#34; data-lang=&#34;cpp&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Tuket&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Rufol&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Muscul&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Jigoro&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;surname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Troco&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Conino&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Man&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Kano&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;unsigned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;age&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;24&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;11&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;78&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;We want to be able to sort these rows by different criterias. For example, we might want to sort by ascending surname alphabetical order:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>BRDF based importance sampling for microfacets cheatsheet</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-07-09-brdf-based-importance-sampling/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 09 Jul 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-07-09-brdf-based-importance-sampling/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p align=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;img src=&#34;http://i.imgur.com/HhN37cN.jpg&#34; width=&#34;60%&#34; height=&#34;60%&#34; alt=&#34;[image]&#34; &gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;BRDF based importance sampling aims to reduce the cost of computing the rendering equation by taking into account the BRDF itself.&#xA;In this case we are going to use the normal distribution function NDF&lt;sup id=&#34;fnref:1&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#fn:1&#34; class=&#34;footnote-ref&#34; role=&#34;doc-noteref&#34;&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;First let&amp;rsquo;s review the rendering equation (simplified version)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;$$&#xA;L_o(\vec{\omega} _ o)= L_e(\vec{\omega} _ o) + \int _\Omega {f_r(\vec{\omega} _i, \vec{\omega} _o) \ L_i(\vec{\omega} _i}) \ (\vec{\omega}_i \cdot \vec{n}) \ d\vec{\omega} _i&#xA;$$&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;$\vec{\omega}_i$: incoming directions.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Curse of Magin</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-07-09-the-curse-of-magin/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 09 Jul 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-07-09-the-curse-of-magin/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;We developed a game for a school subject called the curse of Magin.&#xA;It has been a great experience and very useful for learning.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BZiQbErnMmQ&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;img src=&#34;http://i.imgur.com/my7ICog.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;VIDEO&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Check it out at &lt;a href=&#34;https://thecurseofmagin.itch.io/the-curse-of-magin&#34;&gt;thecurseofmagin.itch.io/the-curse-of-magin&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Create a simple BRDF plotter with Untity</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-05-13-unity-brdf-plotter/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 13 May 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2017-05-13-unity-brdf-plotter/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I needed a tool to plot analytical BRDFs.&#xA;&lt;del&gt;I was surprised I wasn&amp;rsquo;t able to find anything that could fit my requirements&lt;/del&gt;(shame on me: check out &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/wdas/brdf&#34;&gt;the one from Disney&lt;/a&gt;).&#xA;So I created my own interactive BRDF plotter using Unity.&#xA;It&amp;rsquo;s pretty easy to create your own BRDF plotter (if you don&amp;rsquo;t have big expectations on it).&#xA;The goal is to make something useful in a couple hours.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;displacing-vertices-of-a-sphere&#34;&gt;Displacing vertices of a sphere&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What we are going to do is super easy. Create a sphere, evaluate the BRDF, displace the vertices by that amount. We can do this in either the vertex, or geometry shader.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Markdown Guide (modified)</title>
      <link>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2018-08-30-markdown-guide/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://tuket.github.io/posts/2018-08-30-markdown-guide/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;an-h1-header&#34;&gt;An h1 header&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Paragraphs are separated by a blank line.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;2nd paragraph. &lt;em&gt;Italic&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;bold&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;monospace&lt;/code&gt;. Itemized lists&#xA;look like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;this one&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;that one&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;the other one&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note that &amp;mdash; not considering the asterisk &amp;mdash; the actual text&#xA;content starts at 4-columns in.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Block quotes are&#xA;written like so.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;They can span multiple paragraphs,&#xA;if you like.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Use 3 dashes for an em-dash. Use 2 dashes for ranges (ex., &amp;ldquo;it&amp;rsquo;s all&#xA;in chapters 12&amp;ndash;14&amp;rdquo;). Three dots &amp;hellip; will be converted to an ellipsis.&#xA;Unicode is supported. ☺&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
  </channel>
